WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
THE STATE OF MOBILE GAMING:
Remember 5 to 10 years ago when everyone used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:09.240
play mobile games. You had cult like friend groups&nbsp;
that swore by Clash of Clans or Clash Royale or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:15.040
Geometry Dash. They would spend all of their&nbsp;
allowed iPad time or iPod time on these games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.000
and that’s all they would talk about at school and&nbsp;
at hangouts and basically anywhere else they met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.600
up. Don’t ask me how I know. And those were just&nbsp;
the tier 1 games, you also had a slew of tier 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:32.680
games that were sometimes even more popular. These&nbsp;
games didn’t have as dedicated fan bases though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:37.520
They were more of something that you would play on&nbsp;
the bus or at lunch or whenever you had a bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:43.280
free time. I’m talking about games like Temple&nbsp;
Run, Subway Surfers, Flappy Bird, Crossy Road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:49.720
2048, Andry Birds, and so much more that I can’t&nbsp;
even remember because those days are unfortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:55.080
long gone. At first, it seemed like it was just&nbsp;
these OG games that were dying out as they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:01.040
simply run their course. But, the more time that&nbsp;
passed, the more it became clear that mobile games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.920
in general were falling out of favor. Of course,&nbsp;
we’ve seen newer games like Among Us and Fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:12.000
Guys clones have their 5 seconds of fame but none&nbsp;
of these games have had the same sticking power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.200
as the OG games. Here’s how the interest graph&nbsp;
looks for Among Us for example. As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:24.760
it was just one massive spike. For perspective,&nbsp;
here’s how the interest graph for Clash of Clans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:30.280
looks like. It’s much more of a mountain of&nbsp;
interest the lasted multiple years than just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:36.840
singular spike that lasted months. Now, I do want&nbsp;
to note that Geometry Dash has making a comeback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:42.040
out of nowhere which I think is largely due to&nbsp;
extremely high quality videos made by people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:48.400
like Samifying and MathGenius that have reminded&nbsp;
millions of OG players just how fun this game was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.640
But aside from this exception that we don’t even&nbsp;
know how long will last, mobile gaming is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:00.240
much a shell of its former self which is extremely&nbsp;
confusing given that smartphones have just become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:14.265
even more popular throughout all of this. So, why&nbsp;
does no one want to play mobile games anymore?

00:02:14.265 --> 00:02:17.040
THE GLORY DAYS:
To answer why no one plays mobiles games anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.360
we first have to take a look back at what made&nbsp;
mobile games so popular in the first place which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:27.880
I think can be boiled down to 3 reasons starting&nbsp;
with the network effect. None of the games that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:33.080
played as a kid were something that I found on my&nbsp;
own on YouTube or the App Store or something. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:38.440
of the games that I played as a kid were games&nbsp;
that people around me played. People at school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:44.040
people on the bus, people at hangouts, and also&nbsp;
a few dads who wanted to be cool. Nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:49.320
I never see anyone around me playing mobile&nbsp;
games. My only exposure to mobile gaming is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:55.320
through ads from games like Raid Shadow Legends&nbsp;
which is obviously not as effective. This isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:00.640
to say that the network effect ever changed. The&nbsp;
number one reason that people download apps today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:06.040
is also because of people around them. But,&nbsp;
what did change were the type of apps that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.040
benefitted from the network effect. Nowadays,&nbsp;
it’s much more common for a social media app or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:16.920
tv show to benefit from the network effect than a&nbsp;
mobile game. And that brings us into reason number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:23.400
2 which is that mobile games used to have larger&nbsp;
than game goals. Take Clash of Clans for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.960
Progressing in the game wasn’t just about personal&nbsp;
growth or something, it was much more competitive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:34.320
You wanted to be the first guy to get to town&nbsp;
hall 10 or 3000 trophies or beat hexagon force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.320
in your friend group, and that was just on a micro&nbsp;
scale. The most successful games also had larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.320
macro goals. You wanted your friend group to&nbsp;
beat a rival friend group in a clan war or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:50.400
your clan to have more champion league players or&nbsp;
whatever the case may be. Zooming out even more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.320
you wanted your favorite YouTuber to be the one&nbsp;
that won these massive YouTuber Clash of Clans and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:00.680
Clash Royale tournaments. This competitive spirit&nbsp;
probably doesn’t seem like that big of a deal but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:08.760
it made all the difference. Why you ask? Well,&nbsp;
because no game is always 100% fun. With any game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:14.440
there’s high points and low points. But, for these&nbsp;
games with larger than game goals, you didn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:20.760
quit when things got a bit slow or boring because&nbsp;
you still had larger than game goals. You still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.360
had to beat that rival clan, you still had to get&nbsp;
to the legendary league, you still had to beat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.600
Theory of Everything 2. And these macro goals&nbsp;
gave these games an insane amount of sticking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:35.880
power. It wasn’t until entire communities got&nbsp;
bored of a game and its overarching goals that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.648
they would actually die. Speaking of communites,&nbsp;
mobile games back in the day also benefitted from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.648 --> 00:04:39.200
massive YouTube promotions and I’m not talking&nbsp;
about paid promotions. I’m talking about dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.200
YouTube channels that covered these games. People&nbsp;
like Chief Pat, Galadon, Molt, Clash With Ash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:50.960
Orange Juice, BjCanDive, Flammy, and so many more.&nbsp;
Not only did these channels help these games grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:57.040
in popularity but more importantly, they helped&nbsp;
them stay relevant through ups and downs. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.360
sort of community is what has kept games like&nbsp;
Minecraft so popular over the years. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:07.120
a lot of these channels nowadays don’t even&nbsp;
cover mobile gaming anymore or even post for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:13.040
that matter. And we have yet to see a new wave&nbsp;
of YouTubers take their place. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:18.280
there is no such community for Among Us or&nbsp;
Fall Guys or some other new mobile game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:23.320
Combine this lack of community with very little&nbsp;
network effect and no larger than game goals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:28.320
and it’s no wonder why these games have&nbsp;
fallen out of popularity. But all of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:40.817
only tells half the story which brings&nbsp;
us into the developer side of things.

00:05:40.817 --> 00:05:42.360
PREDATORY MONETIZATION:
One of the main reasons that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:48.720
mobile gaming became so popular in the early 2010s&nbsp;
is because they were free to play. As long as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:53.840
had a smartphone or tablet, anyone could download&nbsp;
a mobile game and start playing. This isn’t to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:58.880
say that developers didn’t make any money though.&nbsp;
If your game was super popular and you had super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:04.840
enticing in game purchases like SuperCell, you&nbsp;
could also become super rich. But somewhere along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:10.560
the line, monetization became more important than&nbsp;
the user experience and the enjoyability of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:16.400
game itself. At first, this was just something&nbsp;
that manifested in tier 2 games. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.640
those airplane mode games like Subway Surfers that&nbsp;
filled up time. Initially, the economics of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:27.240
games were simple. You usually had a lite version&nbsp;
of the game and a full version of the game. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:32.200
lite version of the game was usually free and&nbsp;
came with fewer levels and maybe some ads here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:37.560
and there. The ads weren’t all that serious though&nbsp;
as developers weren’t really trying to make money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:42.800
off of ads. Their number one goal with the lite&nbsp;
version was to convince people that the game was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.840
actually fun and worth spending a dollar on to get&nbsp;
the full version, and the full version is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:53.280
they actually made their money. But all of this&nbsp;
changed with the rise of free to play tier 1 games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.360
like Clash of Clans, Clash Royale, Pubg mobile,&nbsp;
Fortnite mobile, and so on. There was like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:03.720
direct inverse correlation between the popularity&nbsp;
of these games and people’s willingness to pay for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:09.360
mobile games. If something as built out as Clash&nbsp;
of Clans could be free, why did they have to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.640
for these stupid tier 2 game. All of a sudden,&nbsp;
trying to convince free users to pay for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:20.880
full version of the game became magnitudes harder.&nbsp;
These developers had to pivot and figure out a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:27.960
to monetize free users and the easiest way to do&nbsp;
that is to just show a bunch of ads. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:32.800
what has led to the app store being filled with&nbsp;
thousands of games that are actually somewhat fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:39.400
but completely unenjoyable because you see an ad&nbsp;
every 30 seconds. As such, one of the main reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:44.200
that mobile gaming has plummeted is because of&nbsp;
corporate efforts to monetize and restrict these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:50.880
games. In the same manner, big banks have long&nbsp;
restricted our saving account interest rates. Of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:58.640
course, the federal funds rate itself was low but&nbsp;
if we’re being honest, the 0.1 or 0.25% rates that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:04.600
banks like Chase and Wells Fargo offered were&nbsp;
just downright disrespectful. The only reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:09.600
they kept it so low was because they knew that we&nbsp;
would keep our money with them anyway because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:16.000
the stability and security they offered. We wanted&nbsp;
to challenge this notion and that’s why we created&nbsp;&nbsp;

