FDIC insurance, please consider checking out Silo&nbsp;
in the description below. But anyway, it wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:10.360
just tier 2 games that made this transition. Tier&nbsp;
1 games would also make this transition. Tier 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:14.720
games didn’t plague their games with a bunch&nbsp;
of ads but what they did do was make the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:20.200
worse and worse for free players. Now of course,&nbsp;
these games were always easier to progress in if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:27.240
you spent money but it was also realistic for a&nbsp;
free player advance and do well in the game. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:33.360
in the second half of the 2010s, this would all&nbsp;
change as these games essentially became pay to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:41.040
win. Clash of Clans for example currently costs&nbsp;
$143,000 to max out. And a recent Clash Royale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:47.640
update increased the cost to max out by over&nbsp;
$14,000 by itself. Back in the day, it didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:53.480
cost that much to max out the entirety of Clash&nbsp;
of Clans. And the worst part is that the stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:59.840
that costs the most money doesn’t add anything to&nbsp;
game play or enjoyabilty. It actually has to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:05.320
with upgrading walls or getting cards from level&nbsp;
14 to level 15, things that add no value to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:10.560
game other than just making these companies more&nbsp;
money. These companies probably thought that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:15.680
were doing the right thing for the company as&nbsp;
revenue continued to go up but they were also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:29.040
simultaneously alienating free players who over&nbsp;
time wanted nothing to do with mobile gaming.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.360
THE X FACTOR:
We’ve clearly seen massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:36.760
changes within the mobile gaming space both on&nbsp;
the community side and on the developer side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:41.280
much of which has been to the detriment of&nbsp;
mobile gaming as a whole. But, while these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:47.240
shifts explain a lot, I think theres actually&nbsp;
an X factor that we have yet to consider which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:52.920
is shifting demographics. I would bet that most&nbsp;
of you guys watching this video are likely in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:58.120
your early to late 20s and are reminiscing about&nbsp;
the good ol days of mobile gaming back in middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:03.400
school and high school. And that’s not surprising&nbsp;
given that the primary demographic that plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:09.040
mobiles games are teenagers. And that’s why it’s&nbsp;
important to consider just how different modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:15.040
teenagers are compared to 10 years ago. Back in&nbsp;
the day, smartphones were just starting to gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:19.840
steam meaning that most of us had to beg our&nbsp;
parents for a smartphone and when we finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:25.680
did get a smartphone it came with a bunch of&nbsp;
caveats. We may have only had a gigabyte of data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:30.720
our phones may have had parental controls enabled,&nbsp;
and maybe we weren’t allowed to use social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.920
apps like Instagram and SnapChat. I remember one&nbsp;
of my elementary school friends, his parents would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:42.360
turn off the wifi at like 8 or 9 because there&nbsp;
was simply no need for internet at night. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:48.200
if he wanted to use his phone after 8 or 9, all&nbsp;
he could do was play offline games like Geometry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:53.480
Dash. Not everyone had the same restrictions but&nbsp;
the restrictions were widespread enough that one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:58.040
of the only things that everyone could do with&nbsp;
their phones together was play family friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:05.200
mobile games. But a lot has changed since then.&nbsp;
Nowadays, smartphone have become a necessity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:09.840
if you’re over the age of 12, you probably&nbsp;
have a smartphone. Parents have also become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:15.280
a lot less strict when it comes to phone usage.&nbsp;
Not only do modern kids have less strict screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:21.160
time but they also have unrestricted access to the&nbsp;
internet and social media. This isn’t to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:28.240
parents have become more irresponsible per se but&nbsp;
simply that there is no other choice. I mean like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:33.440
what are you gonna do, just shelter your kids&nbsp;
from the internet till their 18? First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:39.240
that’s just a massive disadvantage in the modern&nbsp;
age, and second of all, it’s not even possible as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:44.880
kids are more tech savvy than ever and know how&nbsp;
to bypass any and every restriction. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:49.720
the focus has shifted from blocking what teenagers&nbsp;
have access to to teaching them how to be safe and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:55.480
responsible on the internet. In other words, the&nbsp;
average teenager nowadays has access to social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.480 --> 00:13:00.960
media platforms like TikTok, YouTube, Instagram,&nbsp;
and Snapchat and that’s where they spend all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:07.360
their time. Aka, the casual teenage mobile gaming&nbsp;
market, the once largest market for these games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:12.640
has completely disappeared. And the teenagers&nbsp;
that are still interested in playing games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:19.120
usually have console or PC for that leaving&nbsp;
modern mobile games in no man's land. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:24.720
really quite shame because playing these games&nbsp;
were some of my best childhood memories. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:29.880
reality is that the mobile landscape has&nbsp;
massively shifted for gamers, developers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:36.000
and casual smartphone users and that’s why no one&nbsp;
plays mobile games anymore. If you would like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:41.040
help Silo not end up like that, please consider&nbsp;
checking out the link the description below.

